 'Encryption security levels': '\n'
                               '<h2>Encryption &amp; Backup Security Levels</h2><p><b>Start with '
                               'the simplest arrangement that meets your needs.</b> Every extra '
                               'required secret adds a recovery dependency. More layers are not '
                               'automatically better.</p>\n'
                               '<h3>Bitcoin Core wallet encryption</h3><p>Core protects '
                               'private-key material with its wallet passphrase. Public keys, '
                               'addresses, transaction records and other metadata may remain '
                               'readable in wallet.dat. Recovery needs the wallet file; spending '
                               'with an encrypted wallet also needs the Core passphrase. Use '
                               'Wallet Backup to create and verify an encrypted external copy '
                               'before ending the private session.</p>\n'
                               '<h3>One GPG layer — the default</h3><p>A single supported '
                               'wallet.dat is encrypted directly. Other files and folders are '
                               'first packaged in standard TAR. GPG protects the complete payload '
                               'using AES-256 CFB with MDC integrity protection. Recovery requires '
                               'the .gpg file and its passphrase. No GPG keypair is needed. '
                               'Maintain multiple verified copies of the encrypted file and '
                               'independent redundant arrangements for the secret.</p>\n'
                               '<h3>Two or more GPG layers</h3><p>Click <b>+ Add Encryption '
                               'Layer</b>. Each layer needs an independent passphrase, and all '
                               'layers must be opened for recovery. The application supports up to '
                               '16 layers, including 10, but most plans should stay simpler.</p>\n'
                               '<table cellpadding="7" '
                               'border="1"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td>Prepare '
                               'original data</td><td>One wallet.dat, or a standard TAR package '
                               'for other selections</td></tr><tr><td>Encrypt with A</td><td>GPG '
                               'layer 1</td></tr><tr><td>Encrypt that with B</td><td>GPG layer '
                               '2</td></tr><tr><td>Encrypt that with C</td><td>backup.gpg, outer '
                               'layer 3</td></tr></table>\n'
                               '<p>Example: keep redundant encrypted backup copies in two '
                               'locations; keep redundant copies of A with trusted holders in one '
                               'region, B with independent holders in another, and C with '
                               'independent holders in a third. Recovery requires one usable '
                               'encrypted copy and A, B and C. Copies within each group reduce '
                               'loss risk, while separation matters for theft resistance. Consider '
                               'collusion, travel, incapacity and access delays.</p>\n'
                               '<p>An intermediate inner-layer copy bypasses every outer layer. '
                               'Avoid retaining such copies when your plan depends on all layers. '
                               'Keeping all passphrases in one database or on the same device also '
                               "removes the intended separation. The backup file's external name, "
                               'size and timestamps remain visible. Symmetric authentication '
                               'detects damage or tampering; it does not prove who created the '
                               'backup.</p>\n'
                               '<h3>Bitcoin Core plus GPG</h3><p>You may combine Core private-key '
                               'encryption with one or more external GPG layers. Opening every '
                               'external layer reveals wallet.dat and its metadata, but signing '
                               'still needs the separate Core passphrase. Do not count reuse of '
                               'the same passphrase as an additional independent secret. The GUI '
                               'rejects repeated external passphrases; it cannot check the '
                               'internal Core secret.</p>\n'
                               '<h3>Threat models</h3><p>Whole-file encryption provides privacy '
                               'and resistance to theft of a backup without its secrets. '
                               'Redundancy mitigates accidental loss; test every required '
                               'component. Geographic separation can create logistical barriers to '
                               'immediate coercion, but does not guarantee protection from '
                               'continuing coercion or an attacker collecting secrets over time. A '
                               'compromised signing computer may expose keys or every secret '
                               'entered during recovery.</p>\n'
                               '<p>Inheritance needs understandable instructions, accessible '
                               'software, dependable holders and a practiced recovery process. Too '
                               'much complexity can prevent your family from recovering the '
                               'wallet. Match the number of layers to actual independent handling '
                               'and realistic recovery requirements.</p>\n'
                               '<h3>Multiple GPG layers are not multisig</h3><p>GPG layers control '
                               'access to a backup of one wallet. Once opened, that wallet follows '
                               'its ordinary Bitcoin signing rules. Multisig distributes '
                               'transaction authorization across independent Bitcoin keys and '
                               'enforces a signing threshold. Layered backup encryption does not '
                               'create that threshold or distribute signing authorization.</p>\n',
 'Backup & verify': '\n'
                    '<h2>Backup &amp; verify</h2><ol><li>Use <b>Wallet Backup</b> on Home for a '
                    'wallet, or <b>Files → Create Encrypted Backup</b> for selected files and '
                    'folders.</li><li>One GPG layer is the default. Add more only for an explicit '
                    'recovery plan. Generate and confirm a different passphrase for '
                    'each.</li><li>Choose a new .gpg destination in <b>Private Workspace</b>, '
                    'another writable folder or external media. No external drive is required. '
                    'Click <b>Create Encrypted Backup</b>.</li><li>Vault snapshots loaded wallets '
                    'through Bitcoin Core and closed wallets through read-only SQLite backup. One '
                    'supported wallet.dat is encrypted directly; other selections become a TAR '
                    'package in RAM. Vault encrypts and verifies every layer, then decrypts the '
                    'complete result and compares it with the original wallet snapshot or '
                    'package.</li><li>The published encrypted file is checked after copying. A '
                    'direct wallet export is authenticated again and compared with the original '
                    'verified wallet snapshot before its session receipt is saved.</li><li>Copy '
                    'anything you need to keep out of RAM before restarting or shutting down. Make '
                    'independent copies and perform a complete restore/signing drill at least '
                    'annually.</li></ol>\n'
                    '<p>You choose where to save and how to organize or copy your backups. An '
                    'encrypted backup saved only in <b>Private Workspace</b> or another RAM folder '
                    'is lost when the session ends, just like any other file there. Verification '
                    'confirms the backup contents; it does not make RAM storage persistent. '
                    'Restart and shutdown show two warnings followed by a five-minute '
                    'countdown.</p>\n'
                    '<p>Only ciphertext is staged at the destination and publication never '
                    'replaces an existing backup. Originals remain available. Use Wallet Backup '
                    'for a consistent snapshot of each current wallet. Shutdown confirmations do '
                    'not check that your backups exist or are current.</p>\n'
                    '<p>Provisional data remain in verified private RAM with swap forbidden by the '
                    'Vault session policy. The operation is bounded to 16 layers, 10,000 TAR '
                    'entries and at most 256 MiB per package, reduced when free RAM is limited. '
                    'Split larger sets. Cancellation removes temporary data, but this is not a '
                    'claim of secure erasure of Python/Qt memory or physical RAM.</p>\n'
                    '\n'
                    '<h3>Keep the format clear</h3>\n'
                    '<p>A two-layer single-wallet backup commonly ends in '
                    '<b>wallet.dat.gpg.gpg</b>. Opening the outer layer produces <b>inner.gpg</b>; '
                    'opening the last layer produces <b>wallet.dat</b>, with no TAR step. Grouped '
                    'files and folders recover from a TAR payload. Record the required layers in '
                    'your recovery instructions rather than relying on the filename alone.</p>\n'
                    '<p>Vault can still recover older GPG/TAR backups and its previous OCB '
                    'profile. New backups use the current CFB+MDC profile. Test the software you '
                    'intend to use for recovery.</p>\n',
 'Finish Private Session': '\n'
                           '<h2>Finish Private Session</h2><ol><li>Save current wallet backups, '
                           'encrypted databases, required passphrases, notes and signed PSBTs '
                           'outside this RAM session. Keep the required passphrases separately '
                           'recoverable.</li><li>Choose <b>Restart</b> or <b>Finish &amp; Shut '
                           'Down</b>. The first warning explains that anything kept only in RAM '
                           "will be lost. Select <b>I've saved what I need</b> only after saving "
                           'what you need.</li><li>The second warning asks you to confirm the loss '
